intelligence, to robotics, to video games.

57
00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:39.400
It is a rare privilege to be part of a company that isn't just a good business.

58
00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.120
NVIDIA is a good business for sure.

59
00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:44.840
But it's a good business that also makes a great impact on the world.

60
00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.460
And the work that you do is really important to other people.

61
00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:55.580
And so the combination of running a good business and the skills of doing that, I had to learn

62
00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:56.220
along the way.

63
00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:06.300
The purpose of a company that could make such great impact on the industry and markets

64
00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:10.140
around the world and society is a great privilege.

65
00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:13.940
And then also to have done something that the world's never done before and do it for the

66
00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:14.380
first time.

67
00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:20.020
You know, singularly, the company that invented this field of computing, it's just incredible,

68
00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:20.840
incredible fun.

69
00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:23.320
So our first question is on starting NVIDIA.

70
00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:31.100
So in the crowd, we have many aspiring tech entrepreneurs that are close in age when you first founded

71
00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:31.760
NVIDIA.

72
00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:36.720
Can you tell us a bit on how you came to start NVIDIA?

73
00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:40.700
What was the spark that led you to start this amazing journey?

74
00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:47.540
Do you have some advice for some of the entrepreneurs in the crowd who plan on starting their own journeys

75
00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:48.100
one day?

76
00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:53.900
Whatever you do, don't start a graphics company.

77
00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:03.660
NVIDIA being the last, you know, I've always said that NVIDIA was the last computer graphics

78
00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:04.500
company founded.

79
00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:11.000
And that says something about strategy.

80
00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:18.080
You know, we were one of 100 computer graphics companies and also the last one founded.

81
00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:23.600
How is it possible that we're the largest in the world today and the only one remaining?

82
00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:30.180
There's a strategy study in there somewhere.

83
00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:36.580
And that suggests that being first is not necessarily the best.

84
00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.860
You could argue that being the first 99 isn't the best.

85
00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:45.100
And sometimes being last is not necessarily even the worst.

86
00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:52.700
The battle between two computer architecture approaches over the course of time using processors

87
00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:59.400
on the one hand, which is general purpose, writing software that does just anything that runs

88
00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:04.300
on a computer versus an acceleration method has gone on for a very long time.

89
00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:15.040
And about 30 years ago, Chris and Curtis, the two other founders of NVIDIA, wanted to do something

90
00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:15.460
different.

91
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.160
And they wanted me to join them.

92
00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:21.600
I was really happy with my job.

93
00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:25.480
And we had two great kids and a great dog.

94
00:05:25.820 --> 00:05:27.320
And we had a great family.

95
00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:29.640
And so I was perfectly happy doing what we're doing.

96
00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:34.780
But they insisted I join them to do something.

97
00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:41.880
And so the three of us met on a regular basis at Denny's.

98
00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:47.680
And then we conceived of what NVIDIA is today, an accelerated computing company.

99
00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:52.060
And that was really as simple as the beginning as anything.

100
00:05:52.320 --> 00:06:01.140
I don't think that startups need to form out of extraordinary angst or incredible opportunity.

101
00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:07.600
We were three good friends, and we've been good friends today.

102
00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:13.780
And there was an opportunity to work together, and we had to go figure out what to do.

103
00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:19.780
And so I think that entrepreneurial approach to solving problems, looking for opportunities,

104
00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:21.760
persists to this day.

105
00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:24.220
You know, it could be a random Tuesday.

106
00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:30.540
And we're trying to think of something new to go do or something we can do with something that we know.

107
00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:34.380
Or an opportunity were to arise that we can go take advantage of.

108
00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:37.280
And the company is reinventing itself all the time.

109
00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:43.400
So this basic method of seeing problems or seeing opportunities or just happens to be Tuesday,

110
00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:50.920
creating something out of nothing is a skill that I think everybody, every company or every startup needs to have.

111
00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:54.340
We apply that basic method all the time.

112
00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:58.220
We don't have to invent ourselves, reinvent ourselves during a tough day.

113
00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:01.400
We don't have to reinvent ourselves because of an opportunity rise.

114
00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:03.820
We invent ourselves, you know, because we do.

115
00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:12.100
And so just the energy of looking for something new and a new way of doing something is always there.

116
00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:22.180
I still remember it was about February or March 93, and it occurred to us we needed money.

117
00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:26.600
And the place to go get it is venture capitalists.

118
00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:28.900
And the way you do that apparently is to write a business plan.

119
00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:30.400
And so I bought a book.

120
00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:32.300
I mean, the obvious thing to do is you buy a book.

121
00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:35.480
And so I bought a book, and it says how to write a business plan.

122
00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:40.840
Now, back then, how to write a business plan is not a TikTok video.

123
00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:41.620
I wish it was.

124
00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:48.160
And it wasn't even a YouTube video, but it was like 490 pages written by Gordon Bell.

125
00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:51.440
He wrote a book on how to start, how to build a company, how to start a company.

126
00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:53.860
And it was like 490 pages or something like that.

127
00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:59.260
And if I would have read the whole book, there's no way NVIDIA would be in business today.

128
00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:04.980
And so I glanced through it, kind of skimmed through it, and tried to write my business plan.

129
00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:10.240
The truth be told, NVIDIA never finished the business plan because I never could finish it.

130
00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:15.000
And I'm not really even too sure how to finish one today.

131
00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:19.940
And so I do remember this.

132
00:08:20.540 --> 00:08:26.940
Our VC, Sequoia Capital and Sutter, incredible venture capitalists, they asked for a business plan.

133
00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:29.320
And so we put a business plan together.

134
00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:35.580
And I could tell you that our forecast was off by so much.

135
00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:37.620
There's no way to guess even worse than that.

136
00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:40.160
And so we were off by so much.

137
00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:42.380
Nobody would have expected the market to be this large.

138
00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:47.660
Nobody would have expected our company to have taken so long to have gotten off the ground

139
00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:50.140
and for the CEO to take so long to figure it out.

140
00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:54.400
And so all of that stuff was really, really hard to predict.

141
00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:58.200
And so your question is probably, why are we here?

142
00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:01.840
I hope I can figure that out tonight.

143
00:09:02.380 --> 00:09:13.440
There is a belief that CEOs are extraordinary, amazing, really knowledgeable, really skilled people.

144
00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:15.400
And that's just not true.

145
00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:17.080
It is not true.

146
00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:25.420
We're not incompetent, uninformed, really super lazy people either.

147
00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:28.020
I don't want you to think that either.

148
00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:34.620
However, I hope the one thing that I convey to you guys today is that, in fact,

149
00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:38.800
you probably have everything you need to be a great CEO already.

150
00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:45.560
And if there's anything that I inspire you to do tonight, it's just to go do it.

151
00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:47.020
But just don't start a graphics company.

152
00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:49.620
But what was your question?

153
00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:51.280
How did I do it?

154
00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:54.400
It was more like, how did you decide on such a model for NVIDIA?

155
00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:59.500
That question is so loaded and it's hard to answer.

156
00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:01.740
But let me tear it apart.

157
00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:02.620
Okay?

158
00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:03.360
Let me tear it apart.

159
00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:09.120
There's a part about what does the company do?

160
00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:12.200
What does the company do and for what reason it does it?

161
00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:16.040
That's a really, really hard thing to figure out.

162
00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:22.580
And you don't have to figure it out one day or you have the feelings for it,

163
00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:24.440
but you don't know how to put it into words.

164
00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:28.680
And the reason for that is because probably 30 years ago,

165
00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:31.020
the concept of cloud computing didn't exist.

166
00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:36.440
Maybe 30 years ago, nobody would have believed artificial intelligence was possible

167
00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:38.000
or accelerated computing was a thing.

168
00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:42.720
And so until these words come into existence,

169
00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:45.060
it's okay that you don't have the words to describe it,

170
00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:48.980
but you need to know what does the company do and for what reason it does it.

171
00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:58.060
There's the second part, which is in what environment does this company exist?

172
00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:00.300
What kind of environment is it?

173
00:11:00.340 --> 00:11:03.480
Is it a nurturing, stable environment?

174
00:11:04.280 --> 00:11:07.360
Is it a hostile, volatile, dynamic environment?

175
00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:12.120
And because by understanding what environment your company is going to be in,

176
00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:14.720
i.e. the industry and the technology that governs it,

177
00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:16.920
the fundamental dynamics of the industry,

178
00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:18.940
by understanding some of these things,

179
00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:21.820
and that's the reason why if you're in the technology industry,

180
00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:24.220
having technical sensibility is really important.

181
00:11:24.860 --> 00:11:30.660
You can't be ignorant of the fundamental dynamics of the vehicle you're trying to build,

182
00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:33.420
the fleet you're trying to build, the ship you're trying to build.

183
00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:37.720
And so understanding the fundamental dynamics of the industry is really important.

184
00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:39.460
Now based on those dynamics,

185
00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:44.040
it causes you to think about how to architect this machine, this machinery.

186
00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:47.420
This machinery is essentially what NVIDIA is.

187
00:11:48.100 --> 00:11:53.320
NVIDIA is a machine that builds a bunch of other machines that other people would call products.

188
00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:58.040
And so, but it's a machine, and this machine has to be architected in a thoughtful way.

189
00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:00.040
That is the fundamental job of the CEO.

190
00:12:00.860 --> 00:12:03.540
And then the next part of it is how do you operate this machine?

191
00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:07.960
What are the rules that governs the operations of this machine?

192
00:12:07.960 --> 00:12:12.300
Because there will be many conflicting requirements of this machine.

193
00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:17.300
And this machine will be dealing with all of those conflicts in a really distributed way

194
00:12:17.300 --> 00:12:21.220
because you can't possibly be governing everything overnight all by yourself.

195
00:12:21.820 --> 00:12:25.420
And so you have to empower amazing people like René to be able to do some of these things.

196
00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:30.560
And so what are the values that you encode in all of the people that you empowered?

197
00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:32.960
What are the principles by which they operate?

198
00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:37.000
What are the operating manual, if you will, of this machine?

199
00:12:37.640 --> 00:12:39.300
And some people call that culture.

200
00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:45.020
Some people say those are procedures and processes and best known methods.

201
00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:46.940
There are a lot of ways to describe some of these things.

202
00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:50.560
But basically it's what is this thing that we're doing?

203
00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:52.320
What does it do and for what reason?

204
00:12:52.840 --> 00:12:54.980
What is the architecture of the machine and why?

205
00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:56.480
And then how do you operate it?

206
00:12:57.320 --> 00:13:01.220
In a very large sense, all CEOs have to work those things out.

207
00:13:01.220 --> 00:13:06.280
For the vast majority of my computer industry experience,

208
00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:08.880
it's been largely governed by Moore's Law.

209
00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:12.120
But we knew that Moore's Law was going to run out of steam.

210
00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:15.460
And at some point, the type of problems you want to solve,

211
00:13:16.720 --> 00:13:19.260
in our case, the type of problems we want to solve

212
00:13:19.260 --> 00:13:21.240
are the ones that either Moore's Law can't solve

213
00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:26.240
or in the time of Moore's Law, it's barely possible.

214
00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:30.740
And so we came up with this way of doing something called accelerated computing.

215
00:13:30.740 --> 00:13:38.860
Well, our world requires deep invention and a lot of discovery that hasn't been done before

216
00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:40.500
because we're the only company that's done it.

217
00:13:41.460 --> 00:13:44.240
And so you don't know exactly what the recipe is.

218
00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:46.420
You don't know exactly what the framework's going to be.

219
00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:48.160
You don't know exactly how to go do that.

220
00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:55.540
And so we architected a company that optimizes for a lot of innovation to happen,

221
00:13:56.320 --> 00:13:59.180
optimizes for a lot of agility to happen.

222
00:13:59.300 --> 00:14:01.980
So, for example, if we discovered, hey, this is a great new path,

223
00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:05.220
this is going to work, we could swarm the company towards it.

224
00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:07.960
We could shift the company, pivot the company to it.

225
00:14:08.780 --> 00:14:12.700
And to be able to do that, you need a company that allows you to do it.

226
00:14:12.900 --> 00:14:16.340
And these are some of the things that you'll learn as you operate large companies

227
00:14:16.340 --> 00:14:20.880
is that the CEO needs to create a system that allows the CEO,

228
00:14:21.940 --> 00:14:25.040
your will or the collective will of the organization,

229
00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:27.220
to change the course of the company.

230
00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:29.560
And talking about well-run business,

231
00:14:29.860 --> 00:14:32.760
one of your favorite books is The Innovator's Dilemma.

232
00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:35.300
Hopefully, many of you guys have read that book.

233
00:14:35.300 --> 00:14:38.900
And the lesson learned that most people got from that book

234
00:14:38.900 --> 00:14:43.420
is that a well-run business can't afford to switch to a new approach

235
00:14:43.420 --> 00:14:47.740
that ultimately will replace the current business model until it's too late.

236
00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:54.000
